CH.append(("ch01", "Chapter 1. What We Found — the Executive Summary", [
"""Imagine you are a developer dropped into an unfamiliar repository. You open a module and find eleven statements of legal logic. As you read, you notice four things that should not all be true at once in a three-thousand-year-old text. First: the source ships with its own parse tree — every statement carries accent marks that bracket it into a ranked binary tree, and when we extracted those trees mechanically, the brackets landed exactly on the logical joints. Second: every token carries type annotations — a morphology layer marking person, number, gender, voice, and mood — and the legal tradition reads those annotations as load-bearing: a switch from second person to third person routes an obligation from one institution to another. Third: the module imports. Its constants and procedures reference declarations made in earlier modules — the creation week, the covenant of the pieces, the Passover night, the Sinai broadcast — and when we ran a static dependency check against our database of previously derived modules, fifteen of twenty-two declared imports resolved to exact, named locations, and the other seven correctly reported as unresolved externals awaiting modules we have not built yet. Fourth: the module is documented by a spec layer — the oral Torah — that behaves like a combination of specification, linker, standard library, and patch registry. We read all of it: one thousand nine hundred sixty-two texts, every readable word, gate-checked for completeness. Then we compiled what we learned into a running program with thirty-nine sourced claims, and every assertion holds.""",
"""That is the finding in one paragraph. The rest of this report walks the block in sequence — verse one through verse eleven — and for each statement answers the same four questions a code reviewer would ask. What does the statement say? How does its shipped tree split it? What do the morphological types contribute? And what does the spec layer resolve, bind, or override? Along the way, we tag every inheritance: each place the statement consumes something declared in earlier code, with the name of the frozen module our verifier resolved it to.""",
"""A note on notation, since this is meant to be listened to. Hebrew terms appear in transliteration with an immediate English gloss. When I say a word is qal imperfect third person masculine singular, that is the morphology database talking — the tags are in the repository, not in my imagination. When I name a module like gen thirty-one, covenant of the pieces, that is a frozen unit in our corpus, derived and gate-checked before this block was touched. Nothing in this report depends on trusting my reading; everything cited is checkable and most of it is asserted in code that runs.""",
]))

CH.append(("ch02", "Chapter 2. The Toolchain — Four Layers of the Source", [
"""Before the walkthrough, here is the stack, briefly, in developer terms.""",
"""Layer one is the token stream: the consonantal text. Think of it as minified source — extremely compressed, no whitespace decisions, occasionally ambiguous on its own. The compression is real: the Netziv, a nineteenth-century commentator, writes that this portion in particular is written with extreme brevity and that parts of it cannot be parsed at all without the received explanation. That is a source file admitting it requires its spec.""",
"""Layer two is the shipped parse tree: the cantillation marks, the te'amim. Every verse carries accents that are not melody decorations but a ranked bracketing system. The strongest mid-verse mark, the etnachta, is the top-level split — it divides a verse into its two main clauses the way a root node divides an expression. Below it, marks like zaqef and tifcha bracket sub-phrases. Our pipeline derives an explicit binary tree from these marks for every verse, and stores it. The remarkable fact, which this report will demonstrate verse by verse, is that the shipped brackets align with the legal structure: the etnachta consistently falls between a law's condition and its consequence, between its case and its exit clause. The text arrives pre-parsed, and the parse is semantically correct.""",
"""Layer three is the type system: morphology. Every word carries tags — verb stem, aspect, person, gender, number; noun state and possessive suffixes; particles typed as conditionals, negations, object markers. The legal tradition treats these tags as significant to the semantics, not decoration. A second-person verb addresses one party; a third-person verb another — and, as we will see, an entire doctrine about which institution owns which obligation rides on exactly that. A doubled verb form — infinitive absolute plus finite verb — is read as a repetition operator. A singular verb with a compound subject assigns a status bit. The morphology is the API surface.""",
"""Layer four is the spec-and-linker layer: the oral Torah. It does four jobs a modern toolchain would recognize. It resolves overloaded identifiers — the word elohim means both God and judges, and the spec pins which meaning binds at each site. It binds underdetermined constants — 'the seventh' of which clock? The spec supplies the disambiguation rule. It ships the standard library — the parameter tables, the schedules, the worked numeric examples that serve as test vectors. And it holds documented override authority: in a small, explicitly enumerated registry of places, the received law overrides the written token — the text says awl, the law says any instrument — and the tradition maintains the registry of exactly where it has done this, like a patch log. The Vilna Gaon states the doctrine at its most radical: the plain text is the sealing clay, the oral law is the seal, and you must know the plain layer precisely in order to read what was impressed on it.""",
"""One more piece: the corpus is modular. Each derived span of text is a unit — a module — which, once its gates pass, is frozen: its facts become shared state that later modules may consume. Genesis is fully frozen; Exodus is frozen through this chapter; Leviticus only partially. That matters because this block's imports either resolve into frozen modules or correctly fail as forward references. Now, the walkthrough.""",
]))

CH.append(("ch03", "Chapter 3. Verse One — the Module Header and Its Config", [
"""Verse one reads: ve-eleh ha-mishpatim asher tasim lifnehem — and these are the ordinances which you shall set before them. Ten words in English, five tokens in Hebrew. And here is the first structural signal: this is the only verse in the block with no etnachta. The shipped tree has no major mid-verse rest — its top split is a weaker mark, the zaqef, dividing 'and these are the ordinances' from 'which you shall set before them.' Every actual law in this block splits at an etnachta into condition and consequence. This verse does not, because it is not a law. It is the module header. The parser knew.""",
"""Morphology next. The verb tasim is tagged qal imperfect, second person masculine singular: YOU shall set. Every operative verb in the laws that follow is third person — he shall serve, she shall go out. The header is the one place the module speaks to its maintainer directly, which is exactly how a config block behaves. And the tradition noticed the same tag from the other side: a scan source, Pardes Yosef, records that the tav prefix on tasim serves both second person masculine and third person feminine — a dual-gender marker — and hangs on it the rule that women are equated to men for the entire civil code. A gender-parity guarantee derived from a verb prefix. That is the type system doing policy work.""",
"""Now the spec layer, resolving the header's three identifiers. First identifier: ha-mishpatim, the ordinances — with a leading conjunction, ve, and. The spec reads that single letter as a linkage declaration: AND these — appended to what precedes, meaning the Sinai revelation itself; and pointing further back to Marah, the station where, three chapters earlier, the text says a statute and an ordinance were set. The spec's model is two-phase legislation: general principles issued at Marah, details specified here. Our verifier resolves that import to Exodus fifteen, verse twenty-five, inside the frozen module exo fifteen, the song and Marah. The header literally opens by declaring its dependency on an earlier release.""",
"""Second identifier: lifnehem, before them. Before whom? The spec resolves the antecedent to the judges appointed two chapters earlier on Jethro's advice — the court pyramid of thousands, hundreds, fifties, and tens. Our verifier resolves that import to Exodus eighteen, inside frozen module exo eighteen, Jethro and the judges. And the spec attaches the access-control list: before THEM — never before gentile courts, even when their ruling would be identical, because identical output from an unauthorized engine is not the same computation; and not before laymen either, though laymen can be authorized by both parties' consent. Consent can elevate a layman; nothing can authorize the foreign engine. That is a real permission model, with a real distinction between authentication of the judge and authorization of the forum.""",
"""Third resolution: the verb tasim itself, set. Set like what? The spec answers: like a laid table — shulchan arukh — meaning the law must be served ready to eat: taught with its reasons, published to everyone it protects. One scan source states the strongest version: a release-right that has not been published to the man it frees is void. Promulgation is not a nicety; it is a precondition, like an API that is not considered shipped until its documentation is. Another source, the Tur's ancestor text, adds the economic reading: the law is set before them in advance so that no buyer can later claim he did not know the slave serves only six years — published defaults underwrite every price in the market. No ignorance-based rescission.""",
"""Last, the header's position. The verses immediately before it are altar law — you shall not ascend My altar by steps — and the spec reads the adjacency as venue configuration: the high court sits beside the altar, and whatever disqualifies a priest at the altar disqualifies a judge on the bench. Our verifier resolves this edge into frozen module exo twenty, the ten utterances. So before a single law has run, the header has imported its provenance from Marah, its bench from Jethro, its venue from the altar, and configured its permissions. That is a module header by any definition.""",
]))

CH.append(("ch04", "Chapter 4. Verse Two — the First Constructor and an Inherited Constant", [
"""Verse two: ki tiqneh eved ivri, shesh shanim yaavod, u-va-sheviit yetze la-chofshi chinam — when you acquire a Hebrew slave, six years he shall serve, and in the seventh he goes out free, for nothing. The shipped tree splits at the etnachta after yaavod, he shall serve: left branch, the case and the term; right branch, the exit. Condition and consequence, bracketed by the source itself.""",
"""The syntax marker: the verse opens with ki, when. A scan source — the Malbim, systematizing a rule the whole tradition uses — documents the conditional grammar precisely: a multi-branch statute opens its case with ki, and forks its sub-branches with im, if. Watch the block do exactly that: ki opens the slave case here in verse two; verses three, four, and five each open with im — three branches of the switch. Then verse seven opens a new ki — a new case, the daughter — and verses eight through eleven fork with im again. Two switch statements, each with its branches, marked in the source tokens. The same source adds a naming convention: in the God-facing books the person appears before the ki; in the civil code the verb comes first, because interpersonal law is event-driven. This block is verb-first throughout. The convention holds.""",
"""Morphology carries the API boundary. Tiqneh, you shall acquire, is second person: the header's addressee — the court, the buyer under its supervision — is still being spoken to. Then the verse switches: yaavod, he shall serve; yetze, he goes out — third person. A scan source, Rabbi Yerucham Perla, builds the doctrine on exactly this switch: the third-person 'he goes out' means the exit is not the master's discretionary act — it is the court's supervised event; the parallel passage in Deuteronomy, which says 'you shall send him,' addresses the master's own duty. Two verses, two grammatical persons, two different institutions bound. Person-marking as addressee routing.""",
"""Constant binding, the subtle one. The seventh — u-va-sheviit — of what? The word shanah, year, is absent, and the Malbim's rule reads the absence: 'the seventh year' with the noun means the world's calendar; 'the seventh' bare means the seventh of the local counter — this man's own sale clock. So the sabbatical calendar does not free him; his own anniversary does. The tradition then implements the clock precisely: day-for-day years, an edge case for leap years, even an edge where a term ending at a month boundary must stop work a day early because the court might sanctify the new month retroactively. A context-sensitive constant binding, disambiguated by a missing token, implemented with boundary handling.""",
"""Chinam, for nothing, closes the verse: the exit costs nothing and requires nothing — no manumission document, no payment, no reimbursement of the master's medical outlays. The spec types this precisely: term-expiry is automatic; only early status-change requires an instrument. Auto-release at end-of-scope, explicit release before it.""",
"""Now the constant everyone asks about: why six? Nothing inside this verse derives it. It is an import, and our verifier resolves it three times over. The six-and-one frame is declared at creation — six days of work and a seventh set apart — in frozen module gen zero-six. It is re-exported for the manna — gather six days, none falls on the seventh — in frozen module exo sixteen. It is ratified at Sinai — six days shall you labor — in frozen module exo twenty. The slave's term is the third instantiation of the same declared shape, scaled from days to years, and the version-one derivation of this chapter had already tagged it as such. One scan source, Abarbanel, adds the design note: the constant is deliberately not derivable from reason — it is a signature, marking this code as revealed rather than invented. Whatever you make of the theology, as engineering documentation it is exact: term-years equals six is an inherited constant, and the inheritance chain is three frozen modules long.""",
"""The spec layer also guards this constructor with input validation the verse only implies. Sold for the principal, never the penalty multiples. Sold only on witnesses, never on confession. Sold whole or not at all — if the theft is worth less than the man, his labor is garnished and his freedom untouched. And the victim holds a veto: take a note instead. All of it enters the machine as guard clauses, each with its source.""",
]))

CH.append(("ch05", "Chapter 5. Verses Three and Four — Branch Logic, State Snapshots, and a Status Bit", [
"""Verse three: im be-gapo yavo, be-gapo yetze; im baal ishah hu, ve-yatzah ishto imo — if he came in alone, alone he goes out; if he is a husband, his wife goes out with him. The shipped tree splits at the etnachta exactly between the two im branches — the parser brackets the fork. Both branches are entry-state conditionals: the machine reads them as a snapshot taken at construction time. The scan sharpened this into a doctrine: the wife must exist at entry — a marriage contracted later, or ended later, does not flip the branch. State captured at the constructor, immutable for the object's lifetime.""",
"""Notice the repeated noun. Be-gapo appears twice — in his alone-ness he comes, in his alone-ness he goes — and then verse four ends with the same token a third time: ve-hu yetze ve-gapo, and he goes out alone. The Malbim's repeated-noun rule says a re-named noun is a new referent, and the tradition's readings of the three occurrences map exactly: the first pair frames the single man's entry and exit; the third marks the married slave leaving without the bondwoman family. The scan also preserved the type-level dispute on the token itself: gapo from guf, body — he enters with his body and exits with it, which one tannaitic line reads as the rule that no limb-loss buys his freedom — versus gapo from kanaf, garment-corner: he came with only his cloak, meaning unmarried. Both readings are logged as a fork in the machine; the halakhah routes through the second.""",
"""The right branch hides the block's most productive absurdity check. His wife goes out with him — and the Talmud asks the code reviewer's question: goes out? Was she ever IN? She was never sold. The resolution: the verse is telling you the master's ledger carried her — he owed her maintenance for the whole term, which ends, goes out, at exit. From an exit event the spec infers the entry-state obligation. The machine encodes it as the substitution model: the master steps into the husband's slot — feeds the wife, if she is validly his and with him, and the children to age six. Two footnotes show the depth. The children's clause is imported from Leviticus twenty-five, verse forty-one — an unresolved external, as we will see. And the six-year maintenance window is itself derived from this block's term constant: the Chatam Sofer computes that with a fixed six-year term, every child alive at the sale outgrows the six-year support window before the exit — which is exactly why this verse names only the wife, while the Leviticus passage, whose jubilee can interrupt a term early, names the children. Two constants from two books, interlocking so tightly that each passage lists precisely the dependents its own clock leaves possible. That is not prose style. That is consistency across modules.""",
"""Verse four: if his master gives him a wife and she bears sons or daughters, the woman and her children shall be her master's, and he goes out alone. The tree splits at the etnachta between the grant and the consequence. The grant is a gated permission: only to the married slave — and the spec's reason is mechanism design. A single man, given a family in the master's house, will love them and refuse his freedom; the permission would become a retention trap, so it is fenced to men whose first attachment waits outside. One source adds the darker incentive: an unmarried man might sell himself deliberately to obtain the bondwoman. The gate blocks the exploit path.""",
"""And then the status bit, carried by agreement morphology. The consequence clause has a compound subject — the woman AND her children — but the verb tihyeh, shall be, is tagged third person feminine SINGULAR. The Malbim reads the number: the mother is the principal; the children are attributes of her record — they take her status. That is the matrilineal descent rule, the branch of family-status law that says: where no marriage bond can attach, the child follows the mother. Half the lineage algorithm of the legal system hangs off one singular verb with a plural subject. In any other codebase we would call that an overloaded assignment with documented semantics; here the documentation is a chain of sources from the Mekhilta to tractate Kiddushin, all read in the scan.""",
]))

CH.append(("ch06", "Chapter 6. Verses Five and Six — a Repetition Operator and a Procedure Built From Imports", [
"""Verse five: ve-im amor yomar ha-eved — and if the slave shall say, say: I love my master, my wife, my children; I will not go out free. The morphology shows something you can point at: amor is tagged qal infinitive absolute, yomar qal imperfect — the doubled verb form. Biblical Hebrew uses this pairing for emphasis; the legal layer reads it as a repetition count: the declaration must be uttered twice. And the spec binds the timing window with astonishing precision: both sayings must fall while he is still a slave, within the final perutah's-worth of his term — the last coin-sized slice of service. Before the window opens, the saying is void; after it closes, void. A two-shot commit protocol with a validity window, encoded in a verb doubling.""",
"""The declaration's content is a parameter list, and the spec runs an existence check on every parameter: my master, my wife, my children — each must exist, on both sides. The tradition's checklist, encoded in the machine: the slave has wife and children; the master has wife and children; love runs both directions; both parties healthy. Rav Hirsch's essay in the scan reads each check as a hasty-decision guard — a cooling-off design that refuses the irreversible commit unless the current state is genuinely as the declarer describes. A tannaitic fork is preserved — Rabbi Akiva pierces regardless — but the accepted machine gates on the checklist.""",
"""Verse six is the procedure, and the tree brackets its two stages: left of the etnachta, the presentations — and his master shall bring him to ha-elohim, and bring him to the door or to the doorpost; right of the etnachta, the operation — and his master shall pierce his ear with the awl, and he shall serve him le-olam, forever. Note the doubled verb ve-higisho, and he shall bring him — it appears twice in the left branch, and the halakhic decompiler reads the doubling as two distinct stages: stage one at the court, stage two at the door. One medieval source, the Lechem Mishneh, uses exactly this two-stage split to resolve why the doorpost is named at all: door or doorpost are both valid for the standing stage; the piercing itself, stage two, enters the door only. A procedure signature disambiguated by verb repetition.""",
"""Identifier resolution, again: ha-elohim. The token means God in most of the corpus. Here the spec resolves it to the judges — Onkelos, the authorized translation layer, renders it 'before the judges,' and the whole legal stack builds on the resolution: the same court that sold him must witness his refusal of the release it granted. The scan logged the dissents too — Abarbanel insists the word names the jurisdiction rather than the men — and the machine records the fork. Overload resolution with a documented minority report.""",
"""Now the inheritance audit of the procedure's three props, each resolved by the verifier. The instrument: ba-martzea, with THE awl, definite article — and its letters sum to four hundred. Four hundred was declared in frozen module gen thirty-one, the covenant of the pieces: your seed shall serve four hundred years. The man choosing bondage is marked with the number of the term his God cut short — the instrument is a pointer into an earlier declaration, and the verifier resolves it to Genesis fifteen, verse thirteen. The venue: the door and doorpost were the witnesses of the Passover night — the blood on the lintel and posts in frozen module exo twelve, where the nation's ownership was transferred with the words: Mine are the children of Israel as slaves — My slaves, not slaves of slaves. The procedure summons the original witnesses to observe the title being voluntarily re-encumbered. Resolved to Exodus twelve, verse seven — and, a detail we relish, the frozen Passover module's own audit file carries a claim about the spelling of the word doorposts: the prop's provenance is itself under test. The organ: the ear that heard 'do not steal' at Sinai — frozen module exo twenty. The scan preserved the tradition debugging this wire, because the Sinai token in context means kidnapping: three documented resolutions, including the headline doctrine — each commandment was broadcast with its sub-clauses nested inside. Target selection by information history, with the resolution argued in the sources.""",
"""Finally the operator everyone cites: le-olam, forever. The received law binds it to the jubilee — a bounded forever. This is the celebrated entry in the override registry: the tradition itself lists 'with the awl' among the three canonical places where the halakhah overrides the written token, and the Vilna Gaon generalizes: so in most of this portion. But the scan also found the loyal opposition inside the spec: one commentator derives the jubilee bound from the lexicon itself — olam as the fifty-year epoch — no override needed; Rashi calls it a semantic rebinding. Override versus rebinding is a genuine open fork about how the toolchain works, and the machine records it as such. Either way the constant fifty is an import — from Leviticus twenty-five, verse ten. That module is not yet derived. The verifier reports the edge, honestly, as an unresolved external. The pierced man's clock runs on a symbol the linker has not yet seen.""",
]))

CH.append(("ch07", "Chapter 7. Verses Seven and Eight — the Second Constructor and a Two-Channel Token", [
"""Verse seven opens the second case: ve-khi yimkor ish et bito le-amah — and when a man sells his daughter as a maidservant — lo tetze ke-tzet ha-avadim, she shall not go out as the slaves go out. A fresh ki after four im branches: the switch statement resets, the parser and the grammar agreeing that a new case begins. The tree splits at the etnachta between the sale and the not-like-the-slaves clause.""",
"""The constructor's guards are almost entirely spec-supplied, and they are strict. Seller: the father only — ish, a man, excludes the mother; bito, his daughter, excludes the son; and a woman is never sold at all, for her own theft or anyone's. Object: a minor only — and the derivation is a boundary argument the scan recorded in full: the already-sold girl exits at puberty signs; it would be incoherent for the unsold to newly enter past the same boundary. Precondition: total destitution — the father must have sold house, land, and the shirt off his back first; one scan source even finds the rule acrostically stamped in the verse's final letters, which spell 'his inheritance.' Validity condition, the elegant one: the sale is valid only if its designated exit — marriage to the buyer or his son — is legally possible. Sale to a relative, where such marriage is barred, is void. The tradition states it as a principle our machine encodes verbatim: a transaction is valid only with its release path intact. No constructor without a working destructor.""",
"""The clause 'she shall not go out as the slaves go out' is a beautiful case of one line carrying three documented readings, all preserved: she does not exit by mutilation as Canaanite slaves do — she collects damages instead; she is not sent on outdoor errands like a slave — the dignity reading that surfaced, six centuries later, in a responsum about a hired housemaid who refused the market and won; and Maimonides' formal note that the clause is a negation, information rather than command — a statement about the type, not an imperative. Three readings, one token stream, all live in the machine's fork log.""",
"""Verse eight is the crown jewel for anyone who doubts this text behaves like code. It reads: if she is bad in the eyes of her master, asher lo ye'adah — and here the stream carries two channels simultaneously. The written channel, the ketiv, has lo spelled lamed alef: NOT — 'who did NOT designate her.' The read channel, the qere, has lo spelled lamed vav: TO HIM — 'who designated her TO HIMSELF.' Our morphology database carries both tokens side by side at that position — a negation particle and a prepositional phrase — like a source file with a comment preserving the previous revision. The scribal tradition maintains a formal registry of exactly three such alef-to-vav sites in the Torah; this is one, and the masorah says all three come for derivation. The legal layer reads both channels: the read channel gives the rule — designation was the default destiny, and HIS refusal triggers the redemption duty; the written channel, the scan's finest find here, gives HER veto — 'not designated' because she did not consent. Consent symmetry, encoded as a spelling difference between the written and the recited text. Two branches, both compiled.""",
"""The rest of verse eight, right of the etnachta: ve-hefdah — and he shall let her be redeemed — is morphologically causative-flavored: not 'she shall be redeemed' but 'he shall cause it' — the master must actively cooperate. The machine's redemption function implements the spec's arithmetic: a flat per-year deduction at the original purchase rate, though her market value has risen — the tradition says explicitly, do not reappraise her; the master genuinely subsidizes the exit. And if the father regains means, the court forces him to redeem — against his will, for the family's honor. Then the no-resale clause: to a foreign people he shall have no power to sell her, be-vigdo bah — in his betrayal of her. The machine encodes the absorbing state: no resale by anyone, ever; the attempted sale is void. And the inheritance tags land thick here. The one-way status door was exercised by Abraham — after we made Hagar a mistress, we cannot re-enslave her — resolved to frozen module gen thirty-two. The betrayal lexeme is Rachel and Leah's own indictment — he has sold us; are we not counted as foreigners? — resolved to frozen module gen fifty-three. The case-one constructor's national precedent — the nation itself sold for a theft — is the Joseph sale, frozen module gen sixty. And the self-binding cap that Jacob's seven-year contract seems to violate is closed by the mitzvah-for-marriage carve-out, resolved to frozen module gen fifty. Four Genesis modules, four resolved imports, in two verses of Exodus.""",
]))

CH.append(("ch08", "Chapter 8. Verses Nine Through Eleven — Office Inheritance, a Three-Field Record, and Defensive Redundancy", [
"""Verse nine: and if he designates her to his son, ke-mishpat ha-banot yaaseh lah — according to the law of the daughters he shall do for her. Two findings. First, office inheritance: the son steps into the father's designation right, and the original purchase money serves as the son's betrothal money — consideration inherited with the office. The spec bounds the delegation precisely: the son, never the brother — and the formal tie-breaker is counted substitution: the son already stands in for the father in two institutions, the brother in only one. One late formalist, the Rogatchover, derives the whole power from an upstream duty — a father's obligation to marry off his son — which predicts the boundary cases: no grandson designation, no designation by a woman owner. A power derived from a duty, with the derivation constraining the API exactly as documented.""",
"""Second, the null pointer that turned out to be the source of truth. 'The law of the daughters' — ke-mishpat ha-banot — references a body of law that is written nowhere in the Torah. The Mekhilta's own words: this verse came to teach and ended up being taught — the reference is inverted; the amah clause is itself the declaration from which every daughter of Israel's marital rights are derived. In software terms: the call site turned out to be the definition. The general wife-rights law of the entire system is declared here, in the edge case of the sold daughter — and Rav Hirsch's essay names the drafting canon: legislate at the extreme case, where the duty is least expected, and the general case follows a fortiori. The maxim attached: not by one hairbreadth may the beggar's daughter be treated below the free bride.""",
"""Verse ten: if he takes another wife, she'erah kesutah ve-onatah lo yigra — her food, her clothing, and her times he shall not diminish. Morphologically the triad is three construct nouns, each with the same third-person feminine suffix: three fields keyed to one record owner. The machine implements it as exactly that — a three-field record with per-field parameters and per-field flags. Parameters, from the spec's standard library: clothing indexed by age and season — new garments in the rainy season; food at the locale's median, scaled to his wealth, with the ratchet 'she rises with him and does not descend'; the third field scheduled by occupation — the famous table: the idle nightly, sailors at six months, scholars on Sabbath eve — and adjusted for health. Flags: the money fields are waivable by her; the third field is not — body-rights cannot be stipulated away, and a vow cannot defeat the duty because the lien precedes the vow. The scan also preserved the permutation fork faithfully: three tannaitic readings assign the three tokens to the three duties in different orders, and one line reads all three as clothing terms; the psak cascade, remarkably, rides on a single word of the Onkelos translation — sustenance — which a millennium of codes cite as the binding resolution. A one-word translation choice as load-bearing authority: the resolver layer is part of the law.""",
"""Verse eleven: and if these three he does not do for her, ve-yatzah chinam ein kasef — she goes out for nothing; there is no money. The tree splits between the condition and the exit. First, the referent puzzle: 'these three' — which three? The morphology contributes a real signal: shelash is in the construct state — 'the three OF' — pointing backward at named acts, not forward at the triad just listed; and the machine holds the accepted resolution — designation to him, designation to the son, redemption — with the minority triad-reading logged, including its modern rehabilitation: the commentator who derives from it the entire institution of compelled divorce for withheld duties.""",
"""Then the doubled exit, and the block's most self-aware line of documentation. Chinam, for nothing, and ein kasef, no money, look redundant. The Talmud assigns them to two exit events — puberty signs and full majority — and Rashi transmits the reason both needed writing: so as not to give the litigant an opening to distinguish. Read that again as an engineer: the redundancy is defensive — the statute hardened against hostile parsing by a motivated adversary. The spec even preserves the edge case the second clause exists to cover: the aylonit, the girl whose puberty signs never come, who passes directly from minority to majority — without the extra token, her exit would be unreachable. And the negation carries a bonus derivation, the negative-space read: no money to THIS master — but there is money to another master: the father, whose right to betrothal money is derived from the scope of a negation. The opening page of tractate Kiddushin builds the law of marriage acquisition on exactly this inference. A negation's implied complement, load-bearing for another book's first theorem.""",
"""The exits themselves, assembled: her release is a minimum over events — six years, jubilee, signs, the master's death, redemption — whichever fires first; the tradition's own phrasing is the min function in words: whichever precedes, frees her. Except after designation: designation is the absorbing state, the one-way door — she is a wife, and only a divorce document releases her. The machine's state graph is small, explicit, and entirely sourced. And the record's template has its own resolved import: the three-field triad matches, field for field, the vow Jacob makes at Bethel — bread to eat, a garment to wear, and presence — resolved by the verifier to Genesis twenty-eight, verse twenty, frozen module gen forty-eight. The template predates the statute by a book and a half.""",
]))

CH.append(("ch09", "Chapter 9. The Linker Report — Fifteen Resolved, Seven Unresolved, One Caught Lie", [
"""Here is the dependency audit as a linker would print it. The machine file declares twenty-two import edges. A verifier walks each edge against the database of frozen modules — the same index the project's web application serves — and asserts that each edge's declared direction matches reality.""",
"""Fifteen edges resolve. The term constant resolves three times: creation week, module gen zero-six; manna week, module exo sixteen; Sinai's Sabbath, module exo twenty. The piercing procedure resolves three times: the awl's four hundred to the covenant of the pieces, gen thirty-one; the door-and-post witnesses to the Passover night, exo twelve; the hearing ear to the Decalogue, exo twenty. The family files resolve five times: the Joseph-sale precedent, gen sixty; Jacob's seven-year contract, gen fifty; Hagar's irreversible elevation, gen thirty-two; Rachel and Leah's betrayal lexeme, gen fifty-three; Jacob's three-field vow, gen forty-eight. The courtroom resolves three times: Marah's general-principles release, exo fifteen; Jethro's bench, exo eighteen; the altar venue, exo twenty. And one edge resolves that we did not expect — hold that thought for a moment.""",
"""Seven edges do not resolve, and the honesty of the failure is the point. Four point into Leviticus twenty-five, which no unit has derived: the jubilee constant that bounds the pierced man's forever; the self-seller, the other entry path this block's class-split gate references; the children's maintenance verse; and the block's deepest axiom — Mine are the children of Israel as slaves — the prior-lien declaration under the entire module, which one commentator renders in perfect legal English: My document precedes any other document. One points into Leviticus fourteen: the leper's rite that serves the right-ear parameter to three separate procedures — the tradition itself calls the verse deliberately redundant so it can serve as, in our terms, a parameter server. One points into Leviticus twenty-two: the priestly food law whose two category words the Talmud decodes as this block's two slave grades — our type names, load-bearing in another module's law. One points into Deuteronomy fifteen: the severance grant and the diff-style restatement. Seven unresolved externals, each carried on the books as an open demand. When Leviticus twenty-five is derived — it is block seven on the project's own roadmap — four of the seven will link at once, with no edit to this machine.""",
"""And the caught lie. In the first draft of the dependency table, the edge for the designated bondwoman of Leviticus nineteen, verse twenty — the woman defined by this block's verse four — was declared a forward reference. The verifier refused to run: assertion failed; the source verse already lies inside a frozen module, lev nineteen, the holiness duty ledger, derived in an earlier era of the project. The declaration was wrong; the database was right; the table was corrected and the proof re-ran green. A dependency checker that only ever agrees with its author proves nothing. This one disagreed, cited its evidence, and won the argument. That is the moment the audit became credible.""",
]))

CH.append(("ch10", "Chapter 10. The Case — What This Resembles, and What We Claim", [
"""Let me now make the case plainly, as a developer summarizing a code review.""",
"""This block exhibits, verifiably, the following software properties. A header that is structurally distinct from its statements — the only verse without the major mid-verse split — and that configures forum, permissions, promulgation, and venue before any law runs. A shipped parse tree whose top-level brackets align with condition-consequence structure in every statement. A conditional grammar — ki for cases, im for branches — used with complete consistency across two switch statements. Addressee routing by grammatical person, with a documented doctrine hanging on the second-to-third person switch. Constants bound by context rules, including one disambiguated by a missing token. A repetition operator carried by verb doubling, with a specified validity window. Overloaded identifiers resolved by an authorized translation layer whose one-word choices are cited as binding by a thousand years of codes. A two-channel token — written versus read — whose two channels carry two complementary rules, consent his and consent hers. Input validation on both constructors, including a validity condition that voids any sale whose release path is inoperable. A three-field record with per-field parameters and waivability flags. Defensive redundancy documented as such — extra tokens written to defeat hostile parsing. State machines with absorbing states and minimum-over-events exits. Declared module dependencies, an enable flag tied to an institution defined elsewhere, dormancy semantics when that institution lapses, and a patch registry — the halakhah-overrides-the-text list — maintained by the tradition itself, with an internal debate about whether its entries are overrides or lexicon-level rebindings. And a test suite: the tradition's own worked numeric examples, which our implementation passes.""",
"""Against that: the honest caveats. This is a model, and models flatter their makers; the tradition itself contains a minority compilation theory — Ibn Ezra's asmakhta doctrine — on which some derivations are mnemonic indexing rather than mechanical inference, and our machine records that fork rather than resolving it. The dependency edges were chosen by us; what the verifier proves is that each chosen edge lands where we claimed, in a frozen module or outside one — it cannot prove we chose all the edges there are. And morphology tags, trees, and translations pass through human judgment at many points; we inherit their errors.""",
"""But here is what survives every caveat. The eleven verses were read against every text their own tradition wrote about them — one thousand nine hundred sixty-two sources, completeness enforced by a gate, no sampling. The logic extracted from that reading compiled into a program whose assertions all hold. The program's imports were declared and checked mechanically against modules derived months earlier, and the check produced fifteen exact resolutions, seven honest failures, and one correction of its own author. The structural signals — the header without an etnachta, the person switches, the singular verb carrying a status bit, the two-channel spelling, the construct-state numeral — are in the data, retrievable by query, prior to interpretation.""",
"""The claim, then, stated at the strength the evidence bears: read with its own toolchain — its shipped tree, its type layer, its spec — this block behaves like a module in a larger program: it inherits constants, precedents, venues, witnesses, and templates from code declared earlier in the corpus, it declares its unfilled dependencies on code that comes later, and the inheritance graph is not a homiletic flourish but a checkable artifact. The tradition said as much about itself, in its own vocabulary, for two thousand years: general principles at Marah, details at Sinai; the verse as clay, the oral law as seal; this came to teach and ended up taught. We have started writing it down in ours — and it runs.""",
"""End of report. This document describes an experimental computational model of the Torah's legal logic. It is a study artifact, not a work of binding religious law.""",
]))

def xhtml(cid, title, paras):
    body = "\n".join("<p>%s</p>" % p for p in paras)
    return """<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:epub="http://www.idpf.org/2007/ops">
<head><title>%s</title>
<style>body{font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.6;margin:5%%;}
h2{font-size:1.2em;} p{text-align:justify;margin:0 0 1em 0;}</style></head>
<body><h2>%s</h2>
%s
</body></html>""" % (html.escape(title), html.escape(title), body)

with zipfile.ZipFile(OUT, "w") as z:
    z.writestr("mimetype", "application/epub+zip", zipfile.ZIP_STORED)
    z.writestr("META-INF/container.xml", """<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<container version="1.0" xmlns="urn:oasis:names:tc:opendocument:xmlns:container">
<rootfiles><rootfile full-path="OEBPS/content.opf" media-type="application/oebps-package+xml"/></rootfiles>
</container>""")
    manifest, spine, navlis = [], [], []
    for cid, title, paras in CH:
        fn = "%s.xhtml" % cid
        z.writestr("OEBPS/" + fn, xhtml(cid, title, paras))
        manifest.append('<item id="%s" href="%s" media-type="application/xhtml+xml"/>' % (cid, fn))
        spine.append('<itemref idref="%s"/>' % cid)
        navlis.append('<li><a href="%s">%s</a></li>' % (fn, html.escape(title)))
    z.writestr("OEBPS/nav.xhtml", """<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:epub="http://www.idpf.org/2007/ops">
<head><title>Contents</title></head>
<body><nav epub:type="toc" id="toc"><h1>Contents</h1><ol>
%s
</ol></nav></body></html>""" % "\n".join(navlis))
    z.writestr("OEBPS/content.opf", """<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<package xmlns="http://www.idpf.org/2007/opf" version="3.0" unique-identifier="uid">
<metadata xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">
<dc:identifier id="uid">urn:torah-grok:law01:code-report:2026-08-11</dc:identifier>
<dc:title>%s</dc:title>
<dc:language>en</dc:language>
<dc:creator>Torah Grok — the law era, block one</dc:creator>
<meta property="dcterms:modified">%s</meta>
</metadata>
<manifest>
<item id="nav" href="nav.xhtml" media-type="application/xhtml+xml" properties="nav"/>
%s
</manifest>
<spine>
%s
</spine>
</package>""" % (html.escape(TITLE), time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"),
                 "\n".join(manifest), "\n".join(spine)))

words = sum(len(p.split()) for _, _, ps in CH for p in ps)
